1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,760
Right, everyone, let’s make a start.

2
00:00:03,760 --> 00:00:09,994
Over the past few sessions, we’ve been considering the reasons why some world languages

3
00:00:09,994 --> 00:00:11,617
are in decline,

4
00:00:11,617 --> 00:00:15,726
and today I’m going to introduce another factor that affects languages,

5
00:00:15,726 --> 00:00:18,072
and the speakers of those languages,

6
00:00:18,072 --> 00:00:21,880
and that’s technology and, in particular, digital technology.

7
00:00:21,880 --> 00:00:27,231
In order to illustrate its effect, I’m going to focus on the Icelandic

8
00:00:27,231 --> 00:00:27,692
language,

9
00:00:27,692 --> 00:00:34,425
which is spoken by around 321,000 people, most of whom live in Iceland –

10
00:00:34,425 --> 00:00:36,990
an island in the North Atlantic Ocean.

11
00:00:36,990 --> 00:00:41,292
The problem for this language is not the number of speakers – even though

12
00:00:41,292 --> 00:00:42,880
this number is small.

13
00:00:42,880 --> 00:00:47,240
Nor is it about losing words to other languages, such as English.

14
00:00:47,240 --> 00:00:54,143
In fact, the vocabulary of Icelandic is continually increasing because when speakers need a

15
00:00:54,143 --> 00:00:58,778
new word for something, they tend to create one, rather than borrowing from another

16
00:00:58,778 --> 00:00:59,500
language.

17
00:00:59,500 --> 00:01:05,057
All this makes Icelandic quite a special language – it’s changed very little in

18
00:01:05,057 --> 00:01:10,954
the past millennium, yet it can handle twenty-first-century concepts related to the use of computers

19
00:01:10,954 --> 00:01:12,880
and digital technology.

20
00:01:12,880 --> 00:01:18,560
Take, for example, the word for web browser … this is vafri in Icelandic,

21
00:01:18,560 --> 00:01:21,330
which comes from the verb ‘to wander’.

22
00:01:21,330 --> 00:01:26,153
I can’t think of a more appropriate term because that’s exactly what you do

23
00:01:26,153 --> 00:01:27,620
mentally when you browse the internet.

24
00:01:27,620 --> 00:01:32,551
Then there’s an Icelandic word for podcast – which is too hard to pronounce!

25
00:01:32,551 --> 00:01:34,060
And so on.

26
00:01:34,060 --> 00:01:36,288
Icelandic, then, is alive and growing,

27
00:01:36,288 --> 00:01:41,115
but – and it’s a big but – young Icelanders spend a great deal

28
00:01:41,115 --> 00:01:45,570
of time in the digital world and this world is predominantly English.

29
00:01:45,570 --> 00:01:48,240
Think about smartphones.

30
00:01:48,240 --> 00:01:51,352
They didn’t even exist until comparatively recently,

31
00:01:51,352 --> 00:01:56,799
but today young people use them all the time to read books, watch TV

32
00:01:56,799 --> 00:01:59,523
or films, play games, listen to music,

33
00:01:59,523 --> 00:02:00,690
and so on.

34
00:02:00,690 --> 00:02:05,722
Obviously, this is a good thing in many respects because it promotes their bilingual

35
00:02:05,722 --> 00:02:06,082
skills,

36
00:02:06,082 --> 00:02:11,114
but the extent of the influence of English in the virtual world is staggering

37
00:02:11,114 --> 00:02:13,630
and it’s all happening really fast.

38
00:02:13,630 --> 00:02:17,856
For their parents and grandparents, the change is less concerning

39
00:02:17,856 --> 00:02:21,660
because they already have their native-speaker skills in Icelandic.

40
00:02:21,660 --> 00:02:26,790
But for young speakers – well, the outcome is a little troubling.

41
00:02:26,790 --> 00:02:32,322
For example, teachers have found that playground conversations in Icelandic secondary schools can be

42
00:02:32,322 --> 00:02:33,902
conducted entirely in English,

43
00:02:33,902 --> 00:02:39,434
while teachers of much younger children have reported situations where their classes find it

44
00:02:39,434 --> 00:02:44,570
easier to say what is in a picture using English, rather than Icelandic.

45
00:02:44,570 --> 00:02:51,698
The very real and worrying consequence of all this is that the young generation

46
00:02:51,698 --> 00:02:56,790
in Iceland is at risk of losing its mother tongue.

47
00:02:56,790 --> 00:03:00,395
Of course, this is happening to other European languages too,

48
00:03:00,395 --> 00:03:05,443
but while internet companies might be willing to offer, say, French options in their

49
00:03:05,443 --> 00:03:10,851
systems, it’s much harder for them to justify the expense of doing the same

50
00:03:10,851 --> 00:03:15,899
for a language that has a population the size of a French town, such

51
00:03:15,899 --> 00:03:16,620
as Nice.

52
00:03:16,620 --> 00:03:20,102
The other drawback of Icelandic is the grammar,

53
00:03:20,102 --> 00:03:24,020
which is significantly more complex than in most languages.

54
00:03:24,020 --> 00:03:29,090
At the moment, the tech giants are simply not interested in tackling this.

55
00:03:29,090 --> 00:03:33,360
So, what is the Icelandic government doing about this?

56
00:03:33,360 --> 00:03:38,361
Well, large sums of money are being allocated to a language technology fund that

57
00:03:38,361 --> 00:03:42,647
it is hoped will lead to the development of Icelandic sourced apps

58
00:03:42,647 --> 00:03:45,148
and other social media and digital systems,

59
00:03:45,148 --> 00:03:48,720
but clearly this is going to be an uphill struggle.

60
00:03:48,720 --> 00:03:54,685
On the positive side, they know that Icelandic is still the official language of education

61
00:03:54,685 --> 00:03:55,480
and government.

62
00:03:55,480 --> 00:04:00,360
It has survived for well over a thousand years and the experts predict that

63
00:04:00,360 --> 00:04:05,240
its future in this nation state is sound and will continue to be so.

64
00:04:05,240 --> 00:04:11,511
However, there’s no doubt that it’s becoming an inevitable second choice in young people’s

65
00:04:11,511 --> 00:04:11,880
lives.

66
00:04:11,880 --> 00:04:15,140
This raises important questions.

67
00:04:15,140 --> 00:04:19,468
When you consider how much of the past is tied up in a language,

68
00:04:19,468 --> 00:04:22,560
will young Icelanders lose their sense of their own identity?

69
00:04:22,560 --> 00:04:26,430
Another issue that concerns the government of Iceland is this.

70
00:04:26,430 --> 00:04:31,789
If children are learning two languages through different routes, neither of which they are

71
00:04:31,789 --> 00:04:36,000
fully fluent in, will they be able to express themselves properly?
